## DBsubject(Algebra)
## DBchapter(Linear equations and functions)
## DBsection(Linear functions)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Peter Staab)
## Level(1)
## KEYWORDS('graph, lines, linear functions')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'contextFraction.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');
Context("LimitedFraction")->flags->set(showMixedNumbers => 0);

$d1    = random(2, 5);
$n1    = non_zero_random(-1, 1) * random(2, 5);
$d2    = random(2, 5);
$n2    = non_zero_random(-1, 1) * random(2, 5);
$slope = Fraction($n1, $d1);
$yint  = Fraction($n2, $d2);
@y = map { Fraction($n1*$d2*$_ + $n2*$d1, $d1*$d2) } (-2..2);

Context('Fraction');
$f     = Formula("$slope*x+$yint")->reduce;

BEGIN_PGML
Consider the equation [``y=[$f]``].  If [`x=-2,-1,0,1,2`], complete the
following table of points for [`y`]

[#
    [.[`x`].][.[`y`].]*{headerrow => 1}
    [. [`-2`].] [.[_]{$y[0]}.]*
    [. [`-1`].] [.[_]{$y[1]}.]*
    [. [`0`].] [.[_]{$y[2]}.]*
    [. [`1`].] [.[_]{$y[3]}.]*
    [. [`2`].] [.[_]{$y[4]}.]*
#]{padding => 0.25}

For this problem, you need to enter answers in as fractions, not decimals.

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

For each value of [`x`], substitute that value into the equation.  When [`x=-2`],

[`` y = [$slope] (-2)+[$yint] = [$y[0]] ``]

For the remaining values of [`x`], you will get a corresponding [`y`],
which will go into the table.

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();

